/* data/learn-c/phy-12-10.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-10.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-10"] = {"10.1":{"s":"Electric currents and moving charges produce magnetic fields, and a magnetic field pushes on moving charges and on currents. This chapter covers both sides: the force on a charge or a wire in a field, the torque on a current loop (the idea behind motors and galvanometers), and how to find the field of a wire, an arc, a loop, a solenoid and a toroid with the Biot-Savart law and Ampère's law.","f":[{"x":"F = q(v × B)","n":"Magnetic force on a moving charge. Perpendicular to both v and B; does no work."},{"x":"F = BIL sin θ","n":"Force on a current wire"}],"w":["A magnetic force on a charge acts sideways, so it changes the direction of motion but not the speed.","A charge at rest feels no magnetic force."],"x":["In 1820 Hans Christian Oersted saw that a compass needle near a wire turns when a current flows in the wire. This showed that a current produces a magnetic field, and so electricity and magnetism are connected. Within a few weeks Ampere had found that two wires carrying current attract or repel each other.","This chapter has two halves. The first is about the force that a magnetic field exerts on moving charges and on current-carrying wires and loops, including the cyclotron and the moving-coil galvanometer. The second is about the magnetic field that a current produces, using the Biot–Savart law and Ampere's law, for wires, loops, solenoids and toroids.","Two things to keep in mind. The magnetic field B is a vector with the unit tesla (T), and it is defined through the force on a moving charge. Magnetic field lines are closed loops, since there are no isolated magnetic poles, unlike electric field lines, which start and end on charges."],"e":"Earth's magnetic field is about 5 × 10⁻⁵ T. A fridge magnet is about 5 × 10⁻³ T, and a hospital MRI machine is 1.5 to 3 T."},"10.2":{"s":"A charge q moving with velocity v in a magnetic field B feels a force F = q(v × B). The force is perpendicular to both v and B, so it changes the direction of motion but never the speed: a magnetic force does no work. It is largest when v is at right angles to B and zero when v is along B. The SI unit of B is the tesla.","f":[{"x":"F = q*v*B*sin(theta)","n":"Size of the magnetic force; theta = angle between v and B. Direction from v × B (reverse it for a negative charge).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.2, p.231"}],"w":["A charge at rest, or moving along B, feels no magnetic force.","For an electron, find v × B and then reverse the direction."],"x":["A charge q moving with velocity v in a magnetic field B feels the magnetic force F = qv × B. The size is F = qvB sinθ, where θ is the angle between v and B. The direction is at right angles to both v and B, found with the right-hand rule (for a positive charge): point your fingers along v, curl them towards B, and your thumb gives F. For a negative charge the force is opposite.","Notice the special cases. If the charge moves along the field (θ = 0), F = 0. If it moves at right angles to the field, the force is largest, F = qvB. A stationary charge feels no magnetic force.","The force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work and does not change the speed or the kinetic energy of the charge. It changes only the direction of motion. This is a big difference from the electric force, which can speed a charge up.","If both fields are present, the total force is the Lorentz force, F = q(E + v × B). One tesla is defined as the field that exerts a force of 1 N on a charge of 1 C moving at 1 m/s at right angles to the field."],"e":"A charge of 2 μC moving at 5 × 10⁵ m/s at right angles to a field of 0.4 T feels F = 2 × 10⁻⁶ × 5 × 10⁵ × 0.4 = 0.4 N."},"10.3":{"s":"When a charge moves at right angles to a uniform B, the magnetic force is always perpendicular to its velocity, so it moves in a circle at constant speed. The radius grows with momentum, but the time for one turn does not depend on the speed. A cyclotron uses this: two hollow D-shaped electrodes with an alternating voltage at this same frequency give the particle a push every half turn, so it spirals outwards gaining energy.","f":[{"x":"R = m*v/(q*B)","n":"Radius of the circle; m*v = momentum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.3, p.232"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*m/(q*B)","n":"Time for one full circle; the same for slow and fast particles.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.3, p.233"},{"x":"f_c = q*B/(2*pi*m)","n":"Cyclotron frequency; the alternating voltage must have this frequency (resonance).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.3, p.234"},{"x":"KE = q^2*B^2*R^2/(2*m)","n":"Largest kinetic energy a cyclotron of radius R can give.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.3, p.234"}],"w":["The period and frequency do not depend on v or R; only on q, m and B.","The magnetic force does not speed the particle up; the electric field in the gap between the D's does."],"x":["Let a charged particle enter a uniform magnetic field with its velocity at right angles to the field. The magnetic force qvB is always perpendicular to the velocity, with a constant size, so the particle moves in a circle in a plane at right angles to B. The force provides the centripetal force: qvB = mv²/r.","The radius of the circle is r = mv/(qB) = p/(qB). A faster or heavier particle goes in a larger circle, and a stronger field or larger charge makes a smaller circle. The angular speed is ω = qB/m, and the time period is T = 2πm/(qB). The frequency f = qB/(2πm) is called the cyclotron frequency.","Note that T and f do not depend on the speed or on the radius. A faster particle goes in a bigger circle, but in the same time. This is the key to the cyclotron.","The direction of rotation: a positive charge in a field pointing out of the page goes clockwise; a negative charge goes counter-clockwise. In a bubble chamber, the curved tracks of particles in a magnetic field show the sign of the charge and (from the radius) the momentum."],"e":"A proton at 3.0 × 10⁶ m/s in a field of 0.5 T: r = 1.67 × 10⁻²⁷ × 3.0 × 10⁶/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ × 0.5) = 6.26 cm. At 1 T the frequency is 15.25 MHz."},"10.4":{"s":"If the velocity has a part along B as well as a part across it, the two parts behave separately. The part across B makes the charge go round in a circle; the part along B feels no force and stays constant. Together the charge moves on a helix (a spiral) around the field lines, with the same time per turn as in plain circular motion.","f":[{"x":"r = mv/qB ; T = 2πm/qB","n":"Circular motion in B"},{"x":"p = v cos θ × T = 2πm v cos θ / qB","n":"Pitch of a helical path. Charge entering at angle θ to B."}],"w":["Only the velocity part perpendicular to B sets the radius; use v sin θ, not v."],"x":["If the velocity of the particle is not at right angles to the field, split it into two parts: v∥, along B, and v⊥, perpendicular to B. The perpendicular part gives circular motion with radius r = mv⊥/(qB). The parallel part does not feel any force and stays constant. The result is a helix, a spiral path around the field lines.","The pitch of the helix is the distance moved along the field in one revolution: p = v∥ T = v cosθ × 2πm/(qB), where θ is the angle between v and B. The radius is r = mv sinθ/(qB).","In a non-uniform field, which is stronger at the ends, the particle can be reflected, as in a magnetic bottle. Charged particles from the Sun are trapped in this way in the Van Allen radiation belts around the Earth, spiralling along the Earth's field lines from pole to pole.","Near the poles, particles can enter the atmosphere and excite the air, which causes the northern and southern lights (aurora)."],"e":"An electron enters a field at 60° to it, with a speed of 2 × 10⁷ m/s. v⊥ = v sin 60° gives the circle, and v∥ = v cos 60° = 1 × 10⁷ m/s carries it along the field."},"10.5":{"s":"A current is a stream of moving charges, so a wire carrying current in a magnetic field feels a force: the sum of the forces on its drifting electrons. For a straight wire the force is perpendicular to both the wire and B. For a wire of any shape, add up (integrate) the forces on its small pieces I dl × B.","f":[{"x":"F = I*L*B*sin(theta)","n":"Force on a straight wire of length L carrying current I; theta = angle between the wire and B. Vector form F = I L × B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.5, p.235"}],"w":["A wire lying along the field feels no force (θ = 0).","Take the direction of L as the direction of the current, not of electron flow."],"x":["A current is moving charges, so a wire that carries a current in a magnetic field feels a force. For a straight wire of length L carrying a current I, at an angle θ to the field B: F = BIL sinθ, or in vector form F = I L × B, where the vector L points along the current.","The direction is given by Fleming's left-hand rule: stretch the thumb, the first finger and the middle finger of the left hand at right angles to each other. If the first finger points along the field and the middle finger along the current, the thumb gives the direction of the force. The force is largest when the wire is at right angles to the field and zero when it is parallel.","For a wire that is not straight, add up the forces on small elements: dF = I dl × B. In a uniform field, the force on a curved wire between two points is the same as that on a straight wire joining them.","This force is the basis of electric motors, loudspeakers (a coil in a magnet) and moving-coil meters."],"e":"A wire 0.2 m long carrying 5 A at 30° to a field of 0.3 T: F = 0.3 × 5 × 0.2 × sin 30° = 0.15 N."},"10.6":{"s":"For a closed loop of wire in a uniform magnetic field, the small length vectors dl add up to zero around the loop. So the total magnetic force on any closed current loop in a uniform field is zero. The loop is not pushed as a whole, but it can still feel a torque that turns it.","f":[{"x":"F = 0","n":"Net force on any closed current loop in a uniform field.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.6, p.236"}],"w":["Zero net force does not mean zero torque; see the next topic."],"x":["What is the force on a closed loop of wire, for example a rectangular coil, in a uniform magnetic field? Consider each side. Opposite sides have currents in opposite directions, so the forces on them are equal and opposite.","The result is that the net force on any closed loop of current in a uniform field is zero. This can be shown in general: the total force is I(∫dl) × B, and for a closed loop the vector sum ∫dl is zero.","But the loop may feel a torque. The forces on opposite sides may not be along the same line, so they form a couple that turns the loop. This is the subject of the next topic.","In a non-uniform field, the net force on a loop is not zero, and the loop is pulled towards the stronger field (if its moment is along the field) or pushed away (if opposite). This is why a magnet attracts a piece of iron."],"e":"A square loop of side 0.1 m carrying 3 A in a uniform field of 0.2 T: each of the four sides feels F = 0.2 × 3 × 0.1 = 0.06 N, but the four forces add up to zero."},"10.7":{"s":"In a uniform field two sides of a rectangular loop feel equal, opposite forces along different lines: a couple that turns the loop. The torque is largest when the loop's plane is parallel to B and zero when the plane is perpendicular to B. A motor uses this torque. In a moving coil galvanometer a radial field keeps the torque NIAB, a spring balances it, so the deflection is proportional to the current.","f":[{"x":"tau = N*I*A*B*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on a flat coil of N turns, area A; theta = angle between the coil's normal and B. Works for any coil shape.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.7, p.237"},{"x":"phi = (N*A*B/K)*I","n":"Moving coil galvanometer: deflection; K = torsional constant of the spring.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.7, p.238"}],"w":["θ is measured from the NORMAL to the coil, not from the coil's plane.","The radial field keeps sin(θ) = 1 at every deflection, which is why the scale is linear."],"x":["Take a rectangular coil of N turns, with sides a and b (area A = ab), carrying a current I, in a uniform field B. The forces on the two sides that are parallel to the field are zero. The other two sides, of length b, feel equal and opposite forces F = BIb (for each turn), that make a couple.","If the normal to the plane of the coil makes an angle θ with B, the arm of the couple is a sinθ, so the torque is τ = NIAB sinθ. It is largest (NIAB) when the plane of the coil is parallel to the field (θ = 90°) and zero when the plane is perpendicular to the field (θ = 0). The torque turns the coil until its plane is at right angles to the field.","This is the principle of the electric motor and the moving-coil galvanometer. In a motor, a commutator reverses the current every half-turn, so that the torque keeps the same sense and the coil goes on turning.","In a moving-coil galvanometer, a radial field is used (curved pole pieces and a soft iron core), so that the plane of the coil is always parallel to B, and τ = NIAB for every position. A spring gives a restoring torque cθ, so the deflection is proportional to the current: θ = (NAB/c)I."],"e":"A coil of 100 turns, area 0.01 m², carrying 2.0 A in a 0.5 T field has a maximum torque τ = 100 × 2.0 × 0.01 × 0.5 = 1.0 N m."},"10.8":{"s":"A current loop acts like a small magnet, so we give it a magnetic dipole moment μ = NIA. Its direction is along the normal to the loop, chosen by the right hand rule from the current's sense. With μ the torque on the loop in a uniform field is written simply as μ × B, just like the torque p × E on an electric dipole.","f":[{"x":"mu = N*I*A","n":"Magnetic dipole moment of a coil; unit A m² (= J/T).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.8, p.238"},{"x":"tau = mu*B*sin(theta)","n":"Torque on the dipole; theta = angle between mu and B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.8, p.238"}],"w":["Convert the area to m² and the current to A before finding μ."],"x":["A current loop acts like a tiny bar magnet. Its strength is described by the magnetic dipole moment (magnetic moment) m = NIA, where N is the number of turns, I the current and A the area. It is a vector: its direction is along the normal to the loop, given by the right-hand rule (curl the fingers of the right hand along the current, and the thumb shows the direction of m). The unit is A m².","In terms of m, the torque on the loop in a field is τ = m × B, with size mB sinθ, where θ is the angle between m and B. This has the same form as the torque on an electric dipole, τ = p × E.","Atoms have magnetic moments, too. An electron going round the nucleus is a tiny current loop with an orbital moment m = IA = (e/T)πr² = (e/2m_e)L, where L is the orbital angular momentum. For the Bohr orbit, the smallest unit of this is the Bohr magneton μ_B = eh/(4πm_e) = 9.27 × 10⁻²⁴ A m².","A bar magnet of pole strength p and length 2l has a magnetic moment m = p × 2l, pointing from the south pole to the north pole inside the magnet."],"e":"A circular coil of 20 turns and radius 5 cm carrying 3 A has m = NIA = 20 × 3 × π(0.05)² = 0.471 A m²."},"10.9":{"s":"A magnetic dipole in a field has potential energy that depends on its angle to the field, just as an electric dipole does in an electric field. The energy is lowest (most stable) when the dipole moment points along B and highest when it points against B.","f":[{"x":"U = -mu*B*cos(theta)","n":"Potential energy of a magnetic dipole; theta = angle between mu and B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.9, p.239"}],"w":["Work needed to turn the dipole from θ₁ to θ₂ = U₂ − U₁ = μB(cos θ₁ − cos θ₂)."],"x":["A magnetic dipole in a field has a potential energy that depends on its orientation. To turn a dipole from θ₁ to θ₂ against the torque, the work done is the change in energy. Taking zero energy at θ = 90°, the potential energy is U = −mB cosθ = −m · B.","The minimum is U = −mB at θ = 0 (m along B): this is the stable equilibrium, which is why a compass needle lines up with the Earth's field. The maximum is U = +mB at θ = 180° (m against B), an unstable equilibrium: any small disturbance makes the dipole flip.","The work needed to turn a dipole from θ₁ to θ₂ is W = mB(cosθ₁ − cosθ₂). To turn it from the stable position all the way round to the opposite direction needs 2mB.","For small oscillations about the stable position, the torque is about −mBθ, so the dipole performs angular S.H.M. with period T = 2π√(I/(mB)), where I is the moment of inertia. This is used in the vibration magnetometer to compare magnetic fields."],"e":"A bar magnet with m = 0.5 A m² in a field of 0.2 T needs W = 0.5 × 0.2 × (1 − (−1)) = 0.2 J to be turned through 180°, from the stable to the unstable position."},"10.10":{"s":"The Biot-Savart law gives the field made by a small piece of current-carrying wire. The field falls as the inverse square of the distance, grows with the current and the piece's length, and points along dl × r, so it is perpendicular to both. Adding up all the pieces gives the field of a whole wire. For a very long straight wire the field circles the wire and falls as 1/R.","f":[{"x":"dB = (mu_0/(4*pi))*I*dl*sin(theta)/r^2","n":"Field of a current element I dl at distance r; theta = angle between dl and r.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.10, p.240"},{"x":"mu_0 = 4*pi*10^-7 T m/A","n":"Permeability of free space.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.10, p.240"},{"x":"B = mu_0*I/(2*pi*R)","n":"Field at distance R from a very long straight wire.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.10, p.240"},{"x":"B = mu_0*I/(4*pi*R)","n":"Field at perpendicular distance R from the end of a semi-infinite wire (half of the above).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.10, p.240"}],"w":["Points on the line of the wire itself get no field from it (sin θ = 0).","Direction: thumb along the current, fingers curl the way B goes."],"x":["The Biot–Savart law tells the magnetic field produced by a current. A small element of wire of length dl carrying a current I produces, at a point at a distance r from it, a field dB = (μ₀/4π) I dl sinθ/r², where θ is the angle between dl and the line from the element to the point. In vector form dB = (μ₀/4π) I (dl × r̂)/r². Here μ₀ = 4π × 10⁻⁷ T m/A is the permeability of free space.","The direction of dB is perpendicular to the plane of dl and r, given by the right-hand rule. The field is zero along the line of the element itself (θ = 0), and largest at right angles to it.","To find the field of a whole wire, add up (integrate) the contributions of all the elements. For a very long straight wire this gives B = μ₀I/(2πr) at a distance r. The lines of B are concentric circles round the wire, in the direction given by the right-hand thumb rule.","Compare with Coulomb's law: dE = (1/4πε₀) dq/r² falls as 1/r², and so does dB. The difference is the cross-product and the sinθ factor, and that B has closed loops, while E starts on charges."],"e":"A long straight wire carrying 10 A: at 5 cm, B = 2 × 10⁻⁷ × 10/0.05 = 4 × 10⁻⁵ T, close to the size of the Earth's field, so a compass near such a wire is badly affected."},"10.11":{"s":"Each of two long parallel wires sits in the field of the other, so each feels a force. Currents in the same direction attract; currents in opposite directions repel. The forces on the two wires are equal and opposite. The ampere was defined from this force: 1 A in each of two long wires 1 m apart in vacuum gives a force of 2 × 10⁻⁷ N per metre.","f":[{"x":"F/L = mu_0*I_1*I_2/(2*pi*d)","n":"Force per unit length between two long parallel wires d apart.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.11, p.241"}],"w":["Same direction attract, opposite repel: the reverse of the rule for like charges."],"x":["Two long parallel wires carrying currents I₁ and I₂, a distance d apart. The first wire produces a field B₁ = μ₀I₁/(2πd) at the second. The second wire, of length L, carrying I₂, is in this field and feels a force F = B₁I₂L. So the force per unit length is F/L = μ₀I₁I₂/(2πd).","By the right-hand rule, parallel currents (the same direction) attract each other, and antiparallel currents repel. Check that Newton's third law is satisfied: the first wire feels an equal and opposite force from the field of the second one.","This is used to define the ampere. One ampere is the current that, flowing in each of two infinitely long, thin, parallel wires placed 1 m apart in vacuum, produces a force of 2 × 10⁻⁷ N per metre of length between them. (Since 2019, the ampere is fixed in terms of the elementary charge, but the value is the same for practical purposes.) A coulomb is the charge carried by a current of 1 A in 1 s.","Practical cases: the wires of a power line attract or repel during a short circuit, with great force, and a coil of wire (a solenoid) shrinks along its length when a current flows in it, since adjacent turns have parallel currents and attract each other."],"e":"Wires 0.1 m apart carrying 10 A and 20 A in the same direction attract each other with F/L = 2 × 10⁻⁷ × 10 × 20/0.1 = 4 × 10⁻⁴ N/m."},"10.12":{"s":"For a circular arc of radius r, every small piece of wire is at the same distance from the centre and at right angles to the line joining it, so all the pieces add their fields in the same direction. The field at the centre is proportional to the angle the arc subtends. A full circle is the special case with an angle of 2π.","f":[{"x":"B = mu_0*I*theta/(4*pi*r)","n":"Field at the centre of an arc that subtends angle theta (in radians).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.12, p.242"},{"x":"B = mu_0*I/(2*r)","n":"Field at the centre of a full circular loop.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.12, p.242"}],"w":["Put θ in radians: a semicircle has θ = π.","Straight wire parts that point at the centre add no field there."],"x":["For a part of a circle (an arc) of radius R that carries a current I, the Biot–Savart law gives the field at the centre in a simple way. Every element dl of the arc is at the same distance R from the centre, and at right angles to the line to it, so sinθ = 1 and dB = (μ₀/4π) I dl/R². Adding up over the arc of length l = Rφ (where φ is the angle of the arc in radians) gives B = μ₀Iφ/(4πR).","Special cases: a full circle (φ = 2π) gives B = μ₀I/(2R). A semicircle (φ = π) gives B = μ₀I/(4R). A quarter circle gives μ₀I/(8R). For a coil of N turns, multiply by N.","The straight parts of a wire that run radially towards the centre do not contribute, as dl is parallel to r (θ = 0, so sinθ = 0). This makes many problems with a circular arc joined to straight leads easy.","For a combination, add the fields as vectors, with the directions found from the right-hand rule. For example, two semicircles carrying current in opposite senses give fields that cancel at the centre if their radii are equal."],"e":"A semicircular wire of radius 0.1 m carrying 5 A has B at its centre = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 5/(4 × 0.1) = 1.57 × 10⁻⁵ T."},"10.13":{"s":"On the axis of a circular loop the parts of the field across the axis cancel in pairs, so only the part along the axis is left. The field is strongest at the centre and gets weaker as you move away along the axis.","f":[{"x":"B = mu_0*I*R^2/(2*(z^2 + R^2)^(3/2))","n":"Field at distance z from the centre along the axis of a loop of radius R.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.13, p.243"}],"w":["Put z = 0 to get the centre value μ₀I/(2R)."],"x":["For a circular loop of radius R carrying a current I, find the field on the axis, at a distance x from the centre. By symmetry, the components of dB at right angles to the axis cancel in pairs, and only the components along the axis add up.","The result is B = μ₀IR²/(2(R² + x²)^(3/2)) on the axis. At the centre (x = 0) it becomes μ₀I/(2R). For a coil of N turns, multiply by N. Far from the loop (x much larger than R), B ≈ μ₀IR²/(2x³) = μ₀m/(2πx³), where m = IπR². The same form as the field of a bar magnet along its axis.","The direction is along the axis, given by the right-hand rule: the fingers curl with the current, and the thumb points in the direction of B. This is why a loop is a magnetic dipole: it has a north face (where B comes out) and a south face (where B goes in).","The tangent galvanometer uses this: a coil with its plane vertical and along the magnetic meridian, with a small magnet at the centre, and the deflection is given by B tanθ = B_H, so that I = (2RB_H/μ₀N) tanθ."],"e":"A coil of 50 turns and radius 0.1 m carrying 2.0 A has B = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 50 × 2.0/(2 × 0.1) = 6.28 × 10⁻⁴ T at its centre."},"10.14":{"s":"The field lines of a current loop look like those of a short bar magnet: one face of the loop acts as a north pole and the other as a south pole. The right hand rule fixes which is which. Far away along the axis, the loop's field has the same form as an electric dipole's field, with the magnetic moment m = IA in place of p.","f":[{"x":"B = mu_0*N*I/(2*R)","n":"Field at the centre of a flat coil of N turns.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.14, p.244"},{"x":"B = mu_0*m/(2*pi*z^3)","n":"Field far away on the axis (z much larger than R); m = I*A = magnetic moment.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.14, p.244"}],"w":["Far field falls as 1/z³, like a dipole, not as 1/z²."],"x":["The magnetic field lines around a circular current loop have a pattern that is worth knowing. Close to the wire, they are nearly circles around it, like those of a straight wire. Farther away they bend and join, so that through the plane of the loop the lines go through the middle of the loop in one direction, and return around the outside.","Along the axis the lines are straight, in the direction given by the right-hand rule. They are densest near the centre of the loop, and the field falls off along the axis. Seen from far away, the pattern is the same as that of a short bar magnet, with the north pole on the side from which the lines come out.","Properties common to all magnetic field lines: they form closed loops (there are no magnetic monopoles), they do not cross each other, they are closer where the field is stronger, and the tangent to a line at a point gives the direction of B there. The total magnetic flux through any closed surface is zero (Gauss's law for magnetism).","The rule for which face of a loop is north: look at the loop face on. If the current goes anticlockwise, that face is a north pole. If it goes clockwise, it is a south pole."],"e":"A loop seen from above with the current anticlockwise has its north face upwards, so a compass needle placed above it points to the loop's axis, with its north end away from the loop."},"10.15":{"s":"Ampère's law: go round any closed loop, add up the part of B along the loop times each small length, and the total equals μ₀ times the net current passing through the loop. Currents outside the loop add nothing to this total. Like Gauss' law for charges, it gives the field quickly when the current is very symmetric, for example a long straight wire.","f":[{"x":"B*2*pi*r = mu_0*I","n":"Ampère's law on a circle of radius r around a long straight wire; gives B = μ₀I/(2πr).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.15, p.246"}],"w":["Give each current a sign with the right hand rule along the loop's direction.","Ampère's law is always true, but it is only easy to use when B is the same all along a well-chosen loop."],"x":["Ampere's circuital law connects the magnetic field around a closed path to the current through it: ∮B · dl = μ₀I_enclosed. The left side is the sum of B times a small length dl along the path, going once around any closed loop (an 'Amperian loop'). I_enclosed is the net current through the surface bounded by the loop, with the sign from the right-hand rule.","Like Gauss's law in electrostatics, it is always true, but it is most useful where the symmetry allows you to pull B out of the integral. Choose a path along which B has a constant size and is parallel to dl.","For a long straight wire, take a circle of radius r around it: B × 2πr = μ₀I, which gives B = μ₀I/(2πr), the same as from Biot–Savart, with much less work. For a thick wire of radius a carrying a uniform current, B is μ₀Ir/(2πa²) inside (growing with r) and μ₀I/(2πr) outside.","The law is also used for the solenoid and the toroid in the next topic. Maxwell later added a term (the displacement current), so that it works when the electric field changes with time."],"e":"A path that encloses two wires carrying 3 A and 5 A in the same direction has ∮B · dl = μ₀ × 8 A. If the wires carry the currents in opposite directions, the result is μ₀ × 2 A."},"10.16":{"s":"A solenoid is a long, closely wound coil. Inside it the field is nearly uniform and along its axis; outside it is very weak. Ampère's law on a rectangular loop gives B from the turns per unit length and the current. A toroid is a solenoid bent into a ring: its field lines are circles inside the ring, and the field outside it is zero.","f":[{"x":"B = mu_0*n*i","n":"Field inside a long solenoid; n = turns per unit length.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.16, p.247"},{"x":"B = mu_0*N*i/(2*pi*R)","n":"Field inside a toroid of N turns along the circle of radius R.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 10.16, p.247"}],"w":["n is turns per METRE, not the total number of turns.","The solenoid formula is good in the middle; near the ends the field is about half."],"x":["A solenoid is a long coil of wire, wound in a tight helix. When it carries a current, the fields of the turns add up inside and nearly cancel outside. Taking a rectangular Amperian loop with one side inside along the axis and the other side outside, Ampere's law gives, for an ideal long solenoid, B = μ₀nI inside, where n is the number of turns per unit length. The field is uniform and along the axis. Outside, it is very small. At the end of a long solenoid, the field is about half the central value.","The field can be increased by a larger current, more turns per metre, or by a core of iron (a ferromagnetic material), which multiplies the field by a large factor (the relative permeability). Such an electromagnet is used in cranes, relays and electric bells.","A toroid is a solenoid bent into a ring, with N turns altogether. Taking a circular Amperian path of radius r inside it, B × 2πr = μ₀NI, so B = μ₀NI/(2πr) = μ₀nI, with n = N/2πr. The field is confined to the inside of the ring. For paths in the hole or outside, the net current enclosed is zero, so B = 0 there.","A toroid is useful, since the field has no ends and does not spread outside, so it is used in transformers and inductors where the field must be contained."],"e":"A solenoid with 1000 turns per metre carrying 2.0 A has B = 4π × 10⁻⁷ × 1000 × 2.0 = 2.51 × 10⁻³ T inside. With a soft iron core of relative permeability 1000, it would be about 2.5 T."}};
